# CONSULT-64 — Mechanism enumeration & ranking (Opus)

Angle: enumerate candidate mechanisms by which the cell/portal visibility system
drops whole cells' draw calls from *submission* (upstream of any D3D call), healed by
a tiny camera move, at a world↔interior entrance portal, cross-renderer/cross-bitness,
clustered shortly after zone-in, intermittent. Occlusion buffer is ELIMINATED (config-off).

Reasoning is grounded in these engine facts (read, not assumed):

- **Camera cell = stateful segment-crossing history, not a per-frame point test.**
  `RenderWorld::drawScene` roots the whole traversal at `ms_cameraCell =
  camera.getParentCell()` (RenderWorld.cpp:875) → `ms_dpvsCamera->setCell(...)` (:888).
  The camera IS an Object that self-tracks its cell via portal-crossing notifications
  (`RenderWorldCamera` ctor calls `CellProperty::addPortalCrossingNotification(*this)`).
- **The crossing test only runs when the camera moves and integrates a line segment**
  from old→new position against portal quads: `CellPropertyNamespace::Notification::
  positionChanged` (CellProperty.cpp:115-186). `if (start == end) return true;` (:131)
  — a stationary camera is NEVER re-evaluated; its cell is frozen at whatever the last
  segment integration produced. `getDestinationCell` (:720) → `Portal::testPenetration`
  (Portal.cpp:195) is a **segment/triangle collide**, winding-order-signed (:202).
- **dPVS portals are `BACKFACE_CULLABLE` (RenderWorld.cpp:693).** A portal is only
  traversed when the eye is on its front side; an eye on/behind the plane drops it.
- Environment/sky is drawn UNCONDITIONALLY before the dPVS query (:1005-1048,
  `pushCell(worldCell)`), so "sky where interior should be" = interior simply never
  submitted; sky fills the hole. This is why pair B shows sky, not black.

============================================================================
CANDIDATE MECHANISMS
============================================================================

### M1 — Camera containing-cell misresolution (wrong traversal ROOT). RANK 1.
(a) Predicate: `ms_cameraCell` (:875) is the WRONG cell — most damagingly the world
    cell (index 0) when the eye is actually inside the POB, or an interior cell when it
    should be world. dPVS roots at that cell (:888); the entire portal graph is
    traversed from the wrong origin.
(b) Heal by tiny move: the cell is frozen while stationary (:131). ANY position delta
    re-runs `positionChanged`, re-integrates the segment across the entrance portal, and
    corrects `setParentCell`. That is exactly a "small camera change instantly restores."
(c) Consistency: entrance portal ✓ (the one plane the camera's cell hinges on).
    Cross-renderer/bitness ✓ (pure shared sharedObject math, no D3D, no x87 — the 2026-06-20
    FPU_PRESERVE fix was this same neighborhood but a precision variant; x64 SSE is a
    DIFFERENT instance, as the pack states). Post-zone-in cluster ✓✓ (see M1-trigger).
    Intermittent ✓ (depends on the exact segment geometry of one crossing). Strain: none
    material. This is the single best fit to ALL facts.
    - **M1 trigger (why it mis-sets, esp. after zone-in):** the third-person camera
      snaps/pulls-in near the doorway (`cs_cameraPullInSpeed`, door-snap converge, seam-graze
      are all logged camera behaviors right here). A snap is a DISCONTINUOUS jump; the
      old→new segment can tunnel the thin entrance quad, cross it an even number of times,
      or the loop's break guards (`nextCell==currentCell/lastCell`, CellProperty.cpp:164-168)
      can bail mid-chain, leaving the cell one step short. Right after zone-in the camera is
      settling (position set discontinuously, collision converging) → highest snap density →
      the observed cluster. Player cell can be right while the independently-tracked CAMERA
      cell is wrong — nothing forces them equal.
(d) Pair B (160 missing, SKY): root set to world cell; entrance portal into the POB not
    taken → the whole interior subtree + adjacent cells vanish; sky (drawn unconditionally)
    shows through. "Died at the root" = literally the wrong root. Pair A (42 missing, wrong
    wall): root is a CORRECT interior cell but one hop is short/stale, so one neighbor room
    is absent and you see the current cell's back wall — a shallower instance of the same
    misresolution, or a portal-clip (M3/M4) one level down. The two pairs being different
    DEPTHS is the signature this mechanism predicts.

### M2 — dPVS lazy database / cell-bounds not yet committed post-zone-in. RANK 2.
(a) Predicate: the cell's dPVS Cell / PhysicalPortal / member objects exist in the DB but
    the region-of-influence / bounds / import-graph for that cell hasn't been resolved yet
    (objects added via async POB realization), so `resolveVisibility` doesn't yet see them
    inside the cell.
(b) Heal by tiny move: a camera move forces a fresh query + any deferred DB commit /
    dirty-bounds recompute to run that frame → cell appears. (Weaker: many dPVS commits are
    per-query, not per-motion, so a stationary re-query might also fix it — but the user
    only ever perturbs by moving, so indistinguishable in the field.)
(c) Cross-renderer/bitness ✓ (dPVS is renderer-agnostic). Post-zone-in cluster ✓✓ (this is
    fundamentally a "state still settling" mechanism — matches the pack's own open question).
    Entrance portal: WEAK — this would also drop cells with no portal involved; the tight
    clustering AT the entrance is only explained if the entrance POB is the last thing to
    finish realizing. Intermittent ✓ (race with async loader). Strain: doesn't explain "wrong
    WALL through portal" (pair A) as cleanly — that needs a real portal traversal that
    partially succeeded, i.e. M1/M3.
(d) Pair B: whole POB not yet in DB → all its cells missing → sky. GOOD fit for pair B.
    Pair A: only partially explains (one late object/cell).

### M3 — Portal-plane sidedness / winding-order flip within epsilon of the plane. RANK 3.
(a) Predicate: eye is ~on the entrance portal plane; `BACKFACE_CULLABLE` (:693) +
    winding-signed traversal decide the portal is back-facing and skip it, so the neighbor
    cell isn't visited even though the root cell is correct.
(b) Heal by tiny move: crossing to the unambiguous front side flips the sign → portal
    traversed. Tiny delta, instant. Excellent fit to the "heal" clause.
(c) Entrance portal ✓✓ (epsilon-on-plane only happens AT a doorway you're standing in).
    Cross-bitness: PLAUSIBLE but this is the closest cousin to the x87 precision bug already
    fixed once — must call the strain that a pure sidedness-sign flip is more fragile to FP
    than M1's segment integration; on x64 SSE it's less likely but not impossible with a
    genuinely coplanar eye. Post-zone-in cluster: NEUTRAL (sidedness doesn't care about
    load state) — this is M3's main weakness vs M1/M2.
(d) Pair A: root cell correct, ONE portal back-face-rejected → one neighbor room gone,
    back wall shows. TEXTBOOK fit for pair A (42 draws). Pair B: would need MANY portals to
    flip at once — poor fit for 160. So M3 explains A well, B poorly. (Mirror of M2.)

### M4 — Portal-rectangle frustum clip collapses to degenerate area (edge-on / eye inside
     the doorway volume). RANK 4.
(a) Predicate: dPVS projects the portal quad to a screen rectangle and intersects it with
    the running frustum; when the portal is edge-on or the eye is inside the doorway slab,
    the projected rect degenerates to ~zero area / NaN and the portal is pruned as
    "contributes nothing."
(b) Heal by tiny move: rotating/translating gives the quad finite projected area → portal
    kept. Fits heal.
(c) Entrance portal ✓ (edge-on geometry happens at the threshold). Cross-bitness ✓ (integer/
    float rect math in dPVS, renderer-agnostic). Cluster: NEUTRAL (weakness). Overlaps M3 —
    hard to separate from sidedness without a dPVS-internal trace. Strain: dPVS is fairly
    robust to degenerate rects (clamps), so this is less likely than M3 on the same evidence.
(d) Pair A shallow / one portal. Pair B only if the FIRST (entrance) portal degenerates,
    killing the whole subtree — possible, so M4 can reach pair B better than M3.

### M5 — View near-plane straddles the portal quad. RANK 5.
(a) Predicate: near clip plane intersects the entrance portal; the near-plane-clipped
    portal polygon is mis-formed, so the portal is dropped or its sub-frustum is wrong.
(b) Heal: a small pull-back/rotate moves the near plane off the quad. Fits.
(c) Entrance portal ✓, cross-bitness ✓. Cluster NEUTRAL. Strain: the near plane is very
    close to the eye; you'd have to be almost touching the doorway. Subset of M3/M4 spatially.
    Low independent probability.
(d) Similar to M4.

### M6 — Disabled/closed door → portal passability/occluder state. RANK 6 (near-eliminated).
(a) Predicate: door animation/barrier sets the portal disabled or a barrier occluder blocks.
(c) STRAIN/near-refuted: (i) occlusion buffer is OFF, so barrier occluders can't cull; (ii)
    rendering traversal uses geometry portals regardless of `isPassable` for the RENDER path
    — passability gates the crossing test's `passableOnly`, but the cantina entrance is an
    always-open passable portal; (iii) a closed door would be PERSISTENT, not self-clearing
    on a 1° camera nudge. Does not fit "heal by tiny move." Keep only as a ruling-out.
(d) N/A.

### M7 — dPVS write-object-motion / camera-cell desync (camera moved, dPVS cell stale). RANK 7.
(a) Predicate: `ms_dpvsCamera->setCell` uses `ms_cameraCell->getDpvsCell()`; if the cell
    pointer is right but the dPVS camera's internal cell/motion wasn't updated in lockstep
    with the transform, the query roots stale.
(c) Mostly a sub-case of M1 seen from the dPVS side; the code sets cell + matrix together
    each frame (:888-892), so an independent desync is unlikely. Low. Folds into M1.

============================================================================
RANKING (confidence)
============================================================================
1. M1 camera-cell misresolution (wrong root)     — 55%  ← explains EVERY fact incl. cluster + both depths
2. M2 dPVS lazy/uncommitted post-zone-in state    — 20%  ← best "cluster" story; weak on pair A
3. M3 portal sidedness/winding flip (epsilon)     — 12%  ← best pair-A story; weak on cluster + pair B
4. M4 portal-rect degenerate clip                 — 7%
5. M5 near-plane straddles portal                 — 3%
6. M6 door/passability                            — 2%  (near-eliminated)
7. M7 dpvs cell desync                            — 1%  (sub-case of M1)

Note: M1 and M3 are NOT mutually exclusive across the two pairs. The cleanest single
story is **M1 for pair B (root wrong → sky) and M1-or-M3 for pair A (one hop short →
wrong wall).** If forced to one mechanism for both, M1 wins because only it also explains
the post-zone-in clustering (camera snap density) and the total-subtree loss of pair B.

============================================================================
DECISIVE PROBES for the top 2 (one sighting discriminates)
============================================================================
Instrument ONE frame at the sighting; both probes live in `RenderWorld::drawScene`.

PROBE for M1 (and to split M1 vs M3/M4) — the single most decisive log:
  At **RenderWorld.cpp:875-876**, immediately after `ms_cameraCell = camera.getParentCell();`
  log: camera world position; `ms_cameraCell->getCellName()` + `m_cellIndex`; AND the
  player/reference object's parent-cell name+index (whatever object the camera is anchored
  to). THEN at **:1096**, right after `resolveVisibility`, dump `getVisibleCells()`
  (`ms_visibleCellList`) names+count.
  Discrimination in ONE sighting:
   - `ms_cameraCell` is the WORLD cell (idx 0) / disagrees with the player's cell
     → **M1 convicted** (wrong root). Pair-B shape confirmed.
   - `ms_cameraCell` is the CORRECT interior cell BUT the expected neighbor cell is ABSENT
     from `getVisibleCells()` → root was fine, a portal traversal was rejected
     → **M1 refuted, M3/M4 in play** (portal clip/sidedness). Pair-A shape.
  This one paired log cleanly separates "wrong root" (M1) from "right root, portal dropped"
  (M3/M4) — the top structural fork — using state the engine already has in hand.

PROBE for M2 (lazy DB) — discriminate M1 vs M2 when the root IS wrong or a cell is missing:
  Same `getVisibleCells()` dump at :1096 PLUS log whether the missing cell's `CellProperty`
  even has a non-null `getDpvsCell()` and a realized `m_portalObjectList` at sighting time.
   - Missing cell has NULL/empty dPVS cell or no portal objects yet → **M2** (not yet
     committed post-zone-in).
   - Missing cell is fully realized (dpvs cell + portals present) but still absent from the
     visible list while `ms_cameraCell` is correct → **not M2**; portal clip (M3/M4).
  Combined with the M1 probe, a SINGLE captured sighting frame classifies into
  {M1 wrong-root | M2 unrealized | M3/M4 portal-clip}.

Anchor lines: RenderWorld.cpp:875 (root), :888 (setCell), :1093/1096 (query + visible list),
:693 (BACKFACE_CULLABLE portals); CellProperty.cpp:131 (stationary short-circuit),
:162/:720 (crossing test), :181 (setParentCell); Portal.cpp:195/:202 (winding-signed segment).
